        <body>
            <div xml:id="d264">

                <pb facs="0372" n="254" sameAs="frus:frus1929v03/pg254"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">711.9412Anti-War/177</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Japanese Ambassador</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Debuchi</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1929-07-24">July 24, 1929</date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 92</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor, under instructions from
                    my Government, to transmit herewith to you the Declaration of the Imperial
                    Government made on June 27 of this year, concerning the phraseology “in the
                    names of their respective peoples”, appearing in Article 1 of the Treaty for the
                    Renunciation of War, signed at Paris on August 27, 1928. The Declaration was
                    made for the purpose of dispelling any doubt in relation to the Constitution of
                    Japan, elucidating, as it does, the construction placed by the Japanese
                    Government on the phraseology in question.</p>
                <pb facs="0373" n="255" xml:id="pg255"/>
                <p>I am further instructed to request you to be so good as to send a copy of this
                    note and of the Declaration above mentioned to each of the other High
                    Contracting Parties concerned.</p>
                <p>Accept [etc.]</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">K. Debuchi</hi></signed></closer>


                <xi:include href="d264-a1.xml" xpointer="d264-a1"/>
            </div>
